      "text": "To find the vector on the line at \\( t = -7 \\), we need to first determine the parametric equations of the line. The vector on the line at \\( t = 2 \\) is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix}\\), and the vector at \\( t = 3 \\) is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nThe direction vector of the line can be found by subtracting the vector at \\( t = 2 \\) from the vector at \\( t = 3 \\):\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix} - \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 - 1 \\\\ -4 - 4 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -8 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nThis direction vector can be simplified to \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix}\\) by dividing each component by 2.\n\nThe parametric equations of the line can be written as:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} + t \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nThis can be expanded to:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 + t \\\\ 4 - 4t \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nTo find the vector on the line at \\( t = -7 \\), we substitute \\( t = -7 \\) into the parametric equations:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 + (-7) \\\\ 4 - 4(-7) \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 - 7 \\\\ 4 + 28 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -6 \\\\ 32 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nTherefore, the vector on the line at \\( t = -7 \\) is \\(\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -6 \\\\ 32 \\end{pmatrix}}\\).",
