      "text": "To find the vector on the line at \\( t = -7 \\), we need to determine the equation of the line parameterized by \\( t \\). The parametric equations of the line can be written as:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} x_0 \\\\ y_0 \\end{pmatrix} + t \\begin{pmatrix} a \\\\ b \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nGiven:\n- At \\( t = 2 \\), the vector is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n- At \\( t = 3 \\), the vector is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nFirst, let's find the direction vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} a \\\\ b \\end{pmatrix}\\) of the line. The direction vector can be found by subtracting the vector at \\( t = 2 \\) from the vector at \\( t = 3 \\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix} - \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 - 1 \\\\ -4 - 4 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -8 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nSo, the direction vector is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -8 \\end{pmatrix}\\). The slope \\( m \\) of the line is the ratio \\( \\frac{b}{a} \\), which is the negative reciprocal of the direction vector:\n\n\\[\nm = \\frac{-8}{2} = -4\n\\]\n\nNow, we can write the parametric equations of the line as:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} + t \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -8 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 + 2t \\\\ 4 - 8t \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nTo find the vector at \\( t = -7 \\), we substitute \\( t = -7 \\) into the parametric equations:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 + 2(-7) \\\\ 4 - 8(-7) \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 - 14 \\\\ 4 + 56 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -13 \\\\ 60 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the vector on the line at \\( t = -7 \\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -13 \\\\ 60 \\end{pmatrix}}\n\\]",
