      "text": "The vector on the line at $t = 2$ is $\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix},$ and the vector on the line at $t = 3$ is $\\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix}.$\nThe direction vector of the line is $\\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix} - \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -8 \\end{pmatrix}.$\nTo find the vector on the line at $t = -7$, we can use the fact that the vector on the line is a linear combination of the direction vector and the point at $t = 2$.\nLet $\\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix}$ be the vector on the line at $t = -7$.\nThen, \\begin{align*}\n\\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} &= \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} + t \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -8 \\end{pmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{pmatrix} 1 + 2t \\\\ 4 - 8t \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\end{align*}\nTo find the value of $t$ when $t = -7$, we substitute $t = -7$ into the expression for $\\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix}$.\n\\begin{align*}\n\\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} &= \\begin{pmatrix} 1 + 2(-7) \\\\ 4 - 8(-7) \\end{pmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{pmatrix} 1 - 14 \\\\ 4 + 56 \\end{pmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{pmatrix} -13 \\\\ 60 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\end{align*}\nTherefore, the vector on the line at $t = -7$ is $\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -13 \\\\ 60 \\end{pmatrix}}$.The answer is: \\begin{pmatrix}-13\\60\\end{pmatrix}",
